{"name":"Historic White","hex":"#ebe6d7","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"historic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Very important; noteworthy: having importance or significance in history.","Old-fashioned, untouched by modernity."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A history, a non-fiction account of the past.","A historian."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/historic"},{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"}],"entries":[{"title":"History","blocks":[{"text":"History is the systematic study of the past, focusing primarily on the human past. As an academic discipline, it analyses and interprets evidence to construct narratives about what happened and explain why it happened. Some theorists categorize history as a social science, while others see it as part of the humanities or consider it a hybrid discipline. Similar debates surround the purpose of history—for example, whether its main aim is theoretical, to uncover the truth, or practical, to learn lessons from the past. In a more general sense, the term history refers not to an academic field but to the past itself, times in the past, or to individual texts about the past."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Carstian_Luyckx_-_Still_life_with_a_globe%2C_books%2C_shells_and_corals_resting_on_a_stone_ledge.jpg/330px-Carstian_Luyckx_-_Still_life_with_a_globe%2C_books%2C_shells_and_corals_resting_on_a_stone_ledge.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/POxy_v0017_n2099_a_01_hires_-_cropped.jpg/500px-POxy_v0017_n2099_a_01_hires_-_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/POxy_v0017_n2099_a_01_hires_-_cropped.jpg/500px-POxy_v0017_n2099_a_01_hires_-_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fragment of the Histories by Herodotus, an Ancient Greek historical text[24]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APOxy_v0017_n2099_a_01_hires_-_cropped.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Collections_Archive%2C_St._Petersburg_Museum_of_History%2C_St._Petersburg%2C_FL.jpg/500px-Collections_Archive%2C_St._Petersburg_Museum_of_History%2C_St._Petersburg%2C_FL.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Collections_Archive%2C_St._Petersburg_Museum_of_History%2C_St._Petersburg%2C_FL.jpg/1280px-Collections_Archive%2C_St._Petersburg_Museum_of_History%2C_St._Petersburg%2C_FL.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x